  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes light-triggered strategies for controlling delivery of bioactive molecules around a cell. A large central cell is surrounded by yellow-lit regions indicating where illumination acts, and the schemes are divided into two main mechanisms.\n\nOn the left, light causes bond photolysis. In one case, a photocage attached to the cargo is cleaved, releasing the active molecule. In another, a degradable carrier breaks apart under illumination, freeing encapsulated molecules, whereas an intact carrier keeps them trapped before exposure.\n\nOn the right, light drives photoisomerization in materials whose properties can switch reversibly. A hydrophobically stabilized carrier appears to reorganize and release its contents when the photoresponsive groups change state. Similar switching is shown as reversible activation of a ligand–cargo interaction, reversible volume change that alters loading or release, and reversible gating in a porous material where openings can switch between closed and open states.\n\nOverall, the image compares irreversible release by bond cleavage with reversible, tunable control by photoresponsive structural changes. The legend identifies the key components: bioactive molecule, ligand, photocage, photoresponsive link, and illuminated region.",
